 <iati-activity default-currency="USD" hierarchy="1" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="FR" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-BDI202</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Les cadres r&#xe9;glementaire et institutionnel de la protection sociale  sont renforc&#xe9;s</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Despite global progress, access to social protection remains limited in Africa, with only 17.4% of the population benefiting from at least one cash-based scheme. In Burundi, most workers, particularly those in the informal economy, lack coverage, and national data remain incomplete. The current social protection system includes contributory and non-contributory schemes, yet significant gaps persist in both access and effectiveness.
To address these challenges, the ILO supports Burundi through the second phase of the global Flagship Programme on Building Social Protection Floors for All. The programme aims to strengthen universal and sustainable social protection systems across the Great Lakes Region, including in Burundi, Rwanda, and the Democratic Republic of Congo, with regional and global components fostering knowledge sharing and partnerships.
In Burundi, the programme supports the ongoing review of the National Social Protection Policy (PNPS) and its implementation strategy. It also enhances the capacity of national institutions and stakeholders to design, implement, and finance inclusive social protection schemes. By aligning with Burundi&#x2019;s National Development Plan (2018&#x2013;2027) and the ILO Decent Work Country Programme (2020&#x2013;2030), the initiative advocates for social protection as a key tool to reduce poverty, enhance resilience, and promote equitable development.</narrative>
  </description>
